WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.520
Some of it's been spectacular, some of it's
been meh, and some of it's been… well, pretty

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.020
awful.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.800
Still, we can't help but love each and every
iteration of our friendly neighborhood Spider-Man.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.780
That said, we're breaking down every one of
Spidey's flicks ranked worst to best, of course.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:19.700
"What the F..."

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.400
Let's just get this out of the way: Spider-Man
3 features one of the silliest plot points

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.880
in a franchise full of them, and that's the
way the Venom symbiote attaches itself to

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:29.380
Parker.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:34.020
In case you've forgotten — lucky you — it
falls out of space and just up and crawls

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:34.780
to his scooter.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:39.160
Obviously, the comic book version wouldn't
be a viable option, since it would involve

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.020
a massive superhero crossover.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:46.129
But considering Harry Osborn had just taken
over his father's science-based corporation,

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:50.700
it seems like there were plenty of more believable
ways to introduce the insidious oil slick.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.039
"Harry, I need to talk to you — explain
things."

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:58.660
"Tell it to my father.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.720
Raise him from the dead."

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.340
While the overcrowding of villains — including
Sandman, Venom, and the lazily named New Goblin

00:01:06.349 --> 00:01:11.340
— was a chief complaint, perhaps the worst
crime Spider-Man 3 committed was retconning

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.880
the death of Peter's uncle.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.440
Toward the end of the film we learn it was
actually Flint Marko — and not that rando

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.860
Parker let run past him in Spider-Man — who
murdered Ben Parker.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.820
One of the things that makes Spider-Man stand
apart from other heroes is that he actually

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:29.420
is partially to blame for the traumatic death
he tortures himself over.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.140
Pulling out the rug from under that makes
him a very different dude.

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:39.039
Like Spider-Man 3, director Marc Webb's second
Amazing Spider-Man entry had too many bad

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:39.540
guys.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.120
There was Electro, Green Goblin, and the Rhino…
kind of?

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.960
He encounters Spider-Man earlier in the film
as a gunman, but doesn't show up as the Rhino

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.840
literally until the last few minutes.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.320
Rather than the Hulk-sized baddie from the
comics, he's just a dude in a big mech suit,

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.140
and the movie ends abruptly during his battle
with Spider-Man.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.180
It's a pretty anticlimactic moment, to say
the least.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.700
Arguably, it was for the best.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.993
If Amazing Spider-Man 2 hadn't been a failure,
Sony probably wouldn't have been as receptive

00:02:08.993 --> 00:02:13.070
to the idea of partnering with Marvel Studios
and letting ol' webhead come home and finally

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.180
join the MCU.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.430
You couldn't say Andrew Garfield's first shot
as Spidey received universal praise, but it

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.420
was certainly better received than its sequel.

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:26.720
Amazing Spider-Man showed up five years after
Tobey Maguire hung up the tights, and while

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:27.720
it was…

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:33.320
OK, it never quite managed to escape the strangeness
of a franchise rebooted while its predecessor

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.620
was still relatively fresh in the culture's
collective mind.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:40.960
Amazing Spider-Man had to walk a tightrope
of being different enough from the Tobey Maguire

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:45.960
films that it distinguished itself, but not
so different that it became unrecognizable.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:51.400
You can argue back and forth about whether
Garfield or Maguire was better as ol' webhead,

00:02:51.409 --> 00:02:55.519
but there was at least one way in which Garfield's
portrayal simply was superior, and there's

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.840
no argument — he was funny.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.080
"I'm gonna throw you out the window now."

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:01.360
"What?"

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.700
Now, director Sam Raimi's Spider-Man films
had lots of humor, but Maguire's Peter Parker

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.200
was never funny himself.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.640
At least not intentionally.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:18.680
One of Spidey's defining characteristics is
his wisecracking in the face of danger.

00:03:19.189 --> 00:03:22.800
That was something Garfield delivered on,
and it went a long way toward making Amazing

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.740
Spider-Man feel much more like an echo of
the comics.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:30.879
With the glut of superhero movies that we
see today, it's easy to forget how important

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.400
the first Spider-Man was to everything that
followed.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.700
X-Men had been released two years before,
and Blade two years before that.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:43.080
But Blade was a grittier, more violent superhero
and the X-Men had a cool rebel vibe unique

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.060
to the franchise.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.420
And of course they were all dressed in black
leather.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:52.290
Spider-Man was the first 21st century superhero
movie success to feature a more traditional

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.040
crime-fighting hero in bright red and blue.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:00.080
Peter Parker's coming-of-age story proved
that the less edgy superheroes like Iron Man,

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.380
Thor, and Captain America could thrive on
the big screen under the right direction.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:09.100
It was by no means perfect, but it created
a framework for every Spider-Man movie that

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:09.620
followed.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:14.140
Even its mistakes served to improve all the
superhero movies that came after.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.980
Perhaps the biggest fault of Spider-Man is
that the filmmakers went out of their way

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:22.230
to cast the brilliant Willem Dafoe and then
stuck him in a green plastic helmet that made

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.020
him look like a Power Rangers villain.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.980
Every time you notice how dynamic Ryan Reynolds'
mask is in Deadpool or its sequel?

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.620
Those are lessons learned from Spider-Man.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:32.700
Don't doubt it for a second.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.440
Tom Holland is Spider-Man.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.900
With a snarky confidence in his heroic abilities
contrasted with his awkward insecurities and

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:45.470
his desperate need to please Tony Stark he's
got a certain indefinable quality that neither

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.440
of his fellow Peter Parker actors could truly
pin down.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:51.020
"So, uhh, how long you been here?"

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.700
"Long enough to be awkward.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:53.750
Boss wants to see you."

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:55.260
"Is he here too?"

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:56.520
"In the toilet?

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:57.020
No.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:57.520
He's upstate."

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.380
By dialing back Parker's age, we get a much
better look at what it's like for Peter to

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.280
be a superhero and a teenager.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:08.140
And unlike the Maguire Parker, who was almost
completely friendless, Holland has his own

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:12.640
small circle of outcasts — but the fact
that he has friends highlights exactly how

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.540
alone he is when he puts on the suit.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.860
The same can be said of Iron Man's involvement
in the story.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:23.200
Knowing that Tom Holland's Spidey exists in
the same world as Thor and Captain America

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:27.560
makes it feel that much more precarious for
the young hero when he's the only one trying

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.840
to take down Michael Keaton's brilliantly
acted Vulture, even though it's all of those

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.160
other heroes' stuff the guy's trying to steal.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:40.140
Before Tom Hiddleston won audience's hearts
as Loki, few movie supervillains were simultaneously

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:46.770
as ruthless, brutal, and yet somehow sympathetic
as Alfred Molina playing Otto Octavius, a.k.a

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:47.840
Doctor Octopus.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:52.280
Visually, he managed to be both more human
than Willem Dafoe's Green Goblin and more

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:52.780
menacing.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.900
While it may look a little choppy compared
to today's effects, in 2004 the extended battle

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.880
between Doc Ock and Spidey on a moving train
was intense and amazing.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:05.520
As a middle chapter, Spider-Man 2 hit all
the right notes.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.700
The conflict between the life Peter wants
and the one his life as a hero demands, the

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:14.860
revelations Harry Osborn comes to about his
best friend and his father, and the setup

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:16.800
for the next chapter were executed perfectly.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:18.100
Spider-Man

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:22.680
2 felt like a movie made just to show everyone
else exactly what a superhero movie could

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:23.180
be.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.900
There may be a better Spider-Man movie than
Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.580
But if so, it was released in another universe.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:35.800
There are a lot of differences between Into
the Spider-Verse and the 2014 comic book event

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.420
it was loosely based upon.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:42.280
One thing it had in common was the cast of
parallel reality Spider-heroes, and another

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.780
was the delight it took in the medium of comics
and its long history.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.240
"Kingpin knows we're coming.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.060
We're going to be outnumbered."

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.080
"Don't be so sure.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:52.860
You might need these."

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:02.040
there was a cartoon pig Spider-Ham, the anime/manga
inspired SP//dr , Spider-Gwen, and of course

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.120
Nicolas Cage's Spider-Man Noir.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:09.180
"Wherever I go, the wind follows, and the
wind smells like rain…"

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:14.210
Unburdened from live action and from the continuity
restraints of the Marvel Cinematic Universe,

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:19.680
Into the Spider-Verse is action-packed, hilarious,
and kid-friendly while still complex and emotionally

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:21.610
charged enough for adults to enjoy.

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:26.420
It introduced diversity to a franchise without
seeming like it was really trying to do that

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:30.620
— it feels as natural as Spidey swinging
from one building to the next.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:32.040
"I love you, Miles."

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:34.980
"Yeah, I know, Dad."

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:38.170
"You gotta say 'I love you back.'"

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:38.660
"Dad, are you serious?"

